[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Abstract Classes and Interfaces in Depth in TypeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript provides two primary mechanisms for defining contracts and structuring object-oriented applications: Abstract Classes and Interfaces. While they appear similar at first glance, they serve different design purposes and have distinct runtime behaviors."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Abstract Classes"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An abstract class is a base class that cannot be instantiated directly using the 'new' keyword. It is designed to be extended by other classes. Abstract classes can contain both implementation details (concrete methods and properties) and abstract members (signatures without bodies) that must be implemented by subclasses."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"abstract class Department {
  constructor(public name: string) {}

  // Abstract method must be implemented in derived classes
  abstract printMeeting(): void;

  // Concrete method
  printName(): void {
    console.log("Department name: " + this.name);
  }
}

class AccountingDepartment extends Department {
  constructor() {
    super("Accounting and Auditing");
  }

  printMeeting(): void {
    console.log("The accounting department meets each Monday at 10am.");
  }
}

const dept = new AccountingDepartment();
dept.printName();
dept.printMeeting();"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Interfaces"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interfaces define the shape of an object. They are purely a TypeScript compile-time construct and are completely erased during compilation to JavaScript. Interfaces can describe properties, methods, and even index signatures, providing immense flexibility for type-checking."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"interface Describable {
  description: string;
  describe(to: string): void;
}

class Employee implements Describable {
  constructor(public description: string, public name: string) {}

  describe(to: string): void {
    console.log(this.description + " for " + to);
  }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences Between Abstract Classes and Interfaces"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Instantiation: Neither abstract classes nor interfaces can be instantiated directly, but abstract classes can contain constructor logic."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implementation: Abstract classes can provide default implementations for methods. Prior to TypeScript 4.3, interfaces could only declare signatures, though interfaces now support override modifiers."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Inheritance: A class can extend only one abstract class (single inheritance), but it can implement multiple interfaces."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Access Modifiers: Abstract classes support access modifiers (public, protected, private) on their members, whereas all interface members are implicitly public."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"When to Use Which"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Use abstract classes when you want to share code among closely related classes (IS-A relationship) and enforce a common template with some predefined behavior. Use interfaces to define decoupled contracts (CAN-DO relationship) that unrelated classes can implement, promoting loose coupling and maximum flexibility."}]
